WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.887
Unit 1 starts with the
guide's comparison table.

2
00:00:02.887 --> 00:00:08.476
Read it for two things: who manages each
credential, and which kinds speak Kerberos.

3
00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:13.423
A user account used as a service has a
password an administrator sets by hand,

4
00:00:13.423 --> 00:00:17.350
and it's highly exposed to
Kerberoasting when it holds an S P N.

5
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:22.925
An S M S A and a G M S A have a long
random password that Windows manages;

6
00:00:22.925 --> 00:00:28.501
a G M S A's is computed by the domain
controllers from the K D S root key,

7
00:00:28.501 --> 00:00:32.150
and any host allowed to
retrieve it holds it.

8
00:00:32.150 --> 00:00:34.764
A D M S A binds the secret to the machine,

9
00:00:34.764 --> 00:00:39.150
and Microsoft Learn says it's
designed against Kerberoasting.

10
00:00:39.150 --> 00:00:43.934
The Microsoft Entra kinds, a managed
identity, an app registration with

11
00:00:43.934 --> 00:00:48.475
a certificate and a federated
credential, speak OAuth, not Kerberos.

12
00:00:48.475 --> 00:00:50.587
So the table makes two things plain.

13
00:00:50.587 --> 00:00:54.662
Nothing in Microsoft Entra I D
speaks Kerberos for a service.

14
00:00:54.662 --> 00:00:59.210
And a G M S A is a better
password, not a different protocol.
